WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.600
The biggest misconception about
redundancy, and perhaps challenge,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.240
is a natural one and it's that
we all take it personally.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.360
Redundancy is usually just a reality
where organisations are having

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.520
to reorganise and rethink
how they do things.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.440
The more you can focus on
what makes you brilliant

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.160
and then find the next possibility
that matches that brilliance,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.040
the better.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.840
On average in the UK around 40% of us

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.560
will experience redundancy,
at least once in our working lives.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.560
And in my nearly 20 years of work,

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.480
it's already happened to me
a few times.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.520
Start with your strengths.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.720
Your strengths are
what make you distinctive

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.560
and how you add value at work.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.000
And research shows that when we
use our strengths we are six times

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.440
more engaged and generally
more satisfied in our jobs.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.440
If you need some help spotting your
strengths try asking friends, family,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.560
and people you've worked with
this question.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.120
What three words would you use
to describe me at my best?

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.760
This will give you a quick insight
into the positive impact

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.080
that you have on others.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.400
And once you know your strengths,
don't forget to make sure

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.800
they stand out on your CV
and on your LinkedIn profile.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.160
Being made redundant
and searching for new opportunities

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.400
can be tiring, stressful,
and really overwhelming at times.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.480
One useful technique
for staying optimistic

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.240
is to record and reflect
on all your very small successes.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.920
At the end of each day
for at least a week,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.040
write down one success
that you've had.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.160
Perhaps it was just finding the time
to go for a walk

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.840
or supporting a friend in some way
or writing your first blog post.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.720
Even if you've had a tough day,
this exercise helps us to reflect on

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.120
and recognise our positive progress.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.120
Asking for help can feel hard.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.400
So here are a few ideas, prompted by
what I've learned along the way,

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.800
about what works and just as
importantly, what doesn't. 

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.200
First, don't apologize

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.800
and be specific about
the help that you need.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.880
I remember being made redundant

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.680
and asking someone very senior
for career chat.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.680
Within the first minute of our
meeting, she asked me,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.240
"So how can I help you?"

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.400
And I was so grateful for her time

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.520
that I hadn't really thought about
a specific answer to that question.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:17.920
And always avoid using words like

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.440
'sorry' and 'just'
in your messages and conversations,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.640
it's important to be confident in
asking for the help that you need.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.800
Second, think about how you feel
when you're helping someone.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.920
You’re probably flattered and enjoy
the opportunity to be useful.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.160
Remember, that's how
other people feel too.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.960
And finally, if someone can't
help you, don't take it personally.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.720
There's no limit on help,
so ask as many people as possible.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.520
This advice has really helped me
to focus less on what I sometimes

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.320
describe as shiny objects,
like job titles or perks,

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.400
and prioritise making decisions
based on working with people

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.600
and at places
where I can learn the most. 

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.440
I always remember Amanda's advice

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.640
in moments where
I'm feeling overwhelmed.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.560
Fast forwarding to the future
helps me to be more pragmatic

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.680
and prioritise in the here and now.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.000
It's easier than ever to compare our
careers to our family and friends

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.760
and colleagues,
but if we fall into this trap,

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.480
we forget that we're
all motivated by different things.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.720
One of the advantages
of careers today

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.280
is that they can be as individual
as we all are.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.680
So we need to invest in
and take care of our own careers.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.320
I really hope this has been helpful
and very best of luck.

